[Music]  great leaders are committed to  developing other people as leaders which  often means putting them in positions  where they might fail encouraging them  to step outside their comfort zone and  helping them learn from setbacks and  mistakes as opposed to punishing them  for setbacks and mistakes immediately  great leaders also really internalize  the responsibility to embody the culture  and to walk the talk it's a truism in  organizational life that the you know  the sort of the the values plaque you  know the principles that we put up on  the wall is kind of BS and and I think  that's because in a lot of circumstances  people see a big gap between you know  the the plaque on the wall and the  leaders actual behavior they are finely  attuned to moments where the leader is  not acting in alignment with those  principles and so I think great  leadership really means committing to  understanding those principles  internalizing them and and being very  deliberate about following through on  them especially when they don't align  with what the leader believes to be  their self-interest you know if a leader  is going to you know follow the values  on the plaque on the wall  only when it's personally expedient I'd  say that creates a very toxic  environment in which people realize you  know those values in the wall are not  going to be followed they're not ones  that we need to to listen to either  a theme that comes up a lot in my  practice is that leaders occupy a  particularly important symbolic role and  I think this is often neglected in  contemporary organizational life we tend  to think of leaders as you know decision  makers and we think of leaders a very  strategic sense they're going to  determine the course of the organization  and they're going to set strategy and  they're going to allocate resources  that's all true it's also very cognitive  and I think what that leaves out is the  emotional impact that a leader has as  the symbolic leader of the tribe the  symbolic dimension of organizational  life is one that comes up quite a lot in  my practice people join organizations  and feel committed to organizations to a  certain extent because of the feeling of  community that they enjoy among their  colleagues and the commitment that they  feel from the leader to sort of invest  in the health and development to growth  of that community doesn't mean that a  leader is obligated to solve for those  factors but recognizing their leader  bears a unique responsibility to help  that community feel a sense of  collective identity and and growth  great leaders are also able to make a  distinction between leading and doing  many of the people I work with have a a  technical background either as coders or  developers as investors or financial  analysts and they've achieved third  leadership position by virtue of their  technical prowess and that is often very  useful in the early stages of a  company's development because other  people are looking to leader for that  kind of technical expertise and yet if a  leader remains stuck in the process of  doing if they are you know essentially  the doer in chief that if they're if  their leadership rests on their ability  to be the most you know the most  effective you know person at that  particular technical discipline or you  know the proverbial smartest person in  the room  then they're gonna cap the  organization's of ability to grow they  will inhibit the organization's capacity  to scale so you know a transitional  phase that I help a lot of leaders  navigate through is this period of being  the the most technically adept person in  a given discipline to a phase in which  they're actually adding value not on the  basis of their technical expertise  because they're hiring people who have  deeper expertise in their particular  discipline and so the leader is not  adding value by telling that technical  expert how to do their job or by you  know even worse kind of shouldering that  person out of the way and in doing their  job for them but by bringing a group of  technically adept people together and  helping them operate as a team  ultimately that distinction between  weeding and doing is how the leader  truly helps an organization scale  [Music]  you  [Music]  you

